# 45-rate-limiting run 2026-10-04T01:02:14Z
== fresh Postgres for the buckets and the member API (docker compose down -v && up) ==
 Container 45-rate-limiting-postgres-1 Healthy 
setup: tenants (3), api_keys (4), members (3000), buckets, take_tokens(), request_log
== demo: a member API (a separate process) shared by three employers, flooded by one, without then with limits ==

## 1. One bucket, many requests at once
   concept: take_tokens() locks the bucket row, refills it by elapsed time and takes a token in one statement; a read-then-write in the app lets concurrent requests spend the same token
   bucket of 10 tokens, 100 concurrent requests: read-then-write admitted 100, take_tokens() admitted 10
   ok: the row-locked function admits exactly the bucket size
   ok: the unlocked read-then-write admits more than the bucket holds

## 2. Baseline: the quiet tenants alone
   concept: Globex's and Initech's portals at a steady 20 requests a second each, nobody else; this is the latency to protect
   [server] member API on :53055, limits OFF
   client            sent    200    429  429 %  p50 ms  p95 ms  (latency of the 200s, measured by the client)
   acme-batch           0      0      0    0.0       0       0
   acme-sync            0      0      0    0.0       0       0
   globex-portal       60     60      0    0.0      23      27
   initech-portal      61     61      0    0.0      24      26
   quiet tenants (Globex + Initech): p95 27 ms over 121 requests

## 3. Without limits: Acme's batch job floods the shared API
   concept: 48 workers of acme-batch plus 8 of acme-sync, against Globex's and Initech's portals at a steady 20 requests a second each (from second 1 to 11); one pool of 4 connections serves everyone
   client            sent    200    429  429 %  p50 ms  p95 ms  (latency of the 200s, measured by the client)
   acme-batch        1273   1273      0    0.0     227     251
   acme-sync          376    376      0    0.0     226     252
   globex-portal      201    201      0    0.0     229     250
   initech-portal     201    201      0    0.0     228     250
   quiet tenants (Globex + Initech): p95 250 ms over 402 requests

## 4. The limiter's answers: headers on 200 and 429
   concept: every response carries RateLimit-Policy (the quotas) and RateLimit (what is left, and when it is back); a 429 adds Retry-After and a problem+json body
   [server] member API on :53055, limits ON
   GET /members/3 (acme-sync) -> 200
     ratelimit-policy: "tenant";q=50;w=1, "key";q=30;w=1
     ratelimit: "tenant";r=9;t=1, "key";r=5;t=1
   40 requests at once from acme-sync: 7 answered 200, 33 answered 429 (in 110 ms); one of the 429s:
     retry-after: 1
     ratelimit-policy: "tenant";q=50;w=1, "key";q=30;w=1
     ratelimit: "tenant";r=0;t=1, "key";r=0;t=1
     content-type: application/problem+json
     body: {"type":"about:blank","title":"Too Many Requests","status":429,"detail":"quota exceeded for acme-sync, retry after 1 s"}
   ok: the 429 carries Retry-After in whole seconds and both IETF headers
   ok: a burst gets what is left in the key's bucket and no more (at most the burst of 6 + 30/s x 110 ms = 10)

## 5. With limits: the same load
   concept: Acme may use 50 requests a second (burst 10), each of its keys 30 (burst 6); Globex and Initech never come near theirs. The batch job keeps hammering, the sync job waits Retry-After plus jitter
   client            sent    200    429  429 %  p50 ms  p95 ms  (latency of the 200s, measured by the client)
   acme-batch        2489    321   2168   87.1      28      58
   acme-sync          237    167     70   29.5      25      58
   globex-portal      200    200      0    0.0      25      41
   initech-portal     201    201      0    0.0      25      41
   quiet tenants (Globex + Initech): p95 41 ms over 401 requests
   server: 897 served, 154 refused by take_tokens(), 2117 refused from the local memory of the last refusal

## 6. What changed
   concept: the limit moves the queue out of the shared pool and back to the client that caused it
   quiet tenants' p95: 27 ms alone, 250 ms next to Acme without limits, 41 ms with limits
   ok: without limits the quiet tenants' p95 explodes (over 5x their baseline and over 100 ms)
   ok: with limits the quiet tenants stay close to their baseline (under 3x, and under a third of the unlimited run) and never see a 429
   Acme accepted 488 in 11 s (limit 50/s + a burst of 10 = 560); acme-batch 321 (key limit 30/s + 6 = 336)
   ok: Acme gets no more than its quota
   acme-batch: 87.1% of its requests got 429; acme-sync: 29.5%
   ok: the client that hammers keeps getting 429 (over 80% of what it sends, 2.5x the polite client's share)
   ok: the client that honours Retry-After rarely sees one (under 50%) and still gets work done
   acme-sync waited 70 times, 1019..1499 ms (Retry-After 1 s plus up to 50% jitter)
   ok: the polite client's retries are spread out, never earlier than Retry-After
   ok: most refusals never reach Postgres: the local memory of the last refusal answers them

